1
00:00:10,896 --> 00:00:13,299
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a question regarding Kalama Sutta.

2
00:00:13,819 --> 00:00:28,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the famous sutta that's often being quoted, where Buddha encourages people of Kalama, that came to talk to him, to learn how to think basically for oneself.

3
00:00:29,622 --> 00:00:36,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it lists various things to be avoided when it comes to one's own reasoning.

4
00:00:36,425 --> 00:00:44,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that would be contradictory or would constitute what's known as the logical fallacies.

5
00:00:44,689 --> 00:00:44,989
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

6
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:48,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, I'm not going to read the whole Sutta here.

7
00:00:48,151 --> 00:00:49,812
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be included in the description.

8
00:00:50,213 --> 00:01:09,288
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, Kalamās were in doubt, because there were many different teachings, many different teachers, propagating many different teachings, coming around, and then, they couldn't... they were wondering, how can they know for themselves, which one of those teachers is right, and which one isn't.

9
00:01:11,377 --> 00:01:23,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha then tells them that it is quite proper that they are doubtful, because when you're hearing contradictory instructions, you can't say they're all right.

10
00:01:24,104 --> 00:01:31,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to then start... well, you have to then find some sort of criteria that can help you discern which ones

11
00:01:32,068 --> 00:01:39,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, which ones are wrong, or which ones are less likely to be wrong, which ones are more likely to be right, and so on.

12
00:01:39,494 --> 00:01:49,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first one is not to go upon what has been acquired by repeated hearing from different people, i.e.

13
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:49,962
[SPEAKER_00]: majority.

14
00:01:50,502 --> 00:01:50,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

15
00:01:51,623 --> 00:02:02,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to go simply upon tradition, not to go upon hearsay, not to go upon what's in the scriptures, not upon surmise.

16
00:02:03,309 --> 00:02:21,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Not upon an axiom, not upon faulty reasoning, not upon bias, not upon another's apparent competence, competence of another, confidence of another, and not upon mere reasoning, consideration that, oh, this teacher, this monk is our teacher and thus we just go upon what he says.

17
00:02:23,687 --> 00:02:28,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first one is not to go upon what has been acquired by repeated hearing.

18
00:02:28,910 --> 00:02:33,393
[SPEAKER_00]: That's often used as an argument on its own.

19
00:02:33,573 --> 00:02:43,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point of all of these things is, like, not to use these criteria as sole criterias for deciding what's right, what's wrong.

20
00:02:45,561 --> 00:02:51,965
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you have to take responsibility for choosing what criteria you want to use,

21
00:02:52,880 --> 00:03:00,369
[SPEAKER_00]: And therefore, you can't say, oh, this is right because the majority said so, or this is right because of our teacher said so.

22
00:03:00,509 --> 00:03:00,690
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

23
00:03:01,210 --> 00:03:08,880
[SPEAKER_00]: This is right because I chose to regard it as right on the basis of what I heard from my teacher, or on the basis of what majority says.

24
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:10,882
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, majority

25
00:03:11,852 --> 00:03:16,256
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot provide the basis for validity of the argument.

26
00:03:16,376 --> 00:03:20,880
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one choosing to use majority as an excuse for the validity of the argument.

27
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:23,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this case, don't do that.

28
00:03:23,342 --> 00:03:27,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't go upon that which has been acquired by repeated hearing.

29
00:03:27,926 --> 00:03:37,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've been hearing the same thing here, and same thing there, and same thing somewhere else, on and on and on and on, through like many different sources, but you've been hearing the same thing.

30
00:03:37,814 --> 00:03:43,277
[SPEAKER_00]: And, for some people, that will be enough to take that thing to be the truth.

31
00:03:44,257 --> 00:04:00,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, the point here, that it's useful to add, is that sometimes, when people come across the conflicting instructions, they will choose to side with instruction that came from the majority.

32
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:01,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply,

33
00:04:01,704 --> 00:04:08,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the majority of people saying, and thus, all these people are more likely to be right than this one person who says that.

34
00:04:09,849 --> 00:04:16,152
[SPEAKER_00]: But that in itself, as I said, it's a it's a it's a plain logical fallacy, because it's not about how many people are saying something.

35
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:21,675
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about whether that thing has the universal truth in it.

36
00:04:21,755 --> 00:04:24,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Universal truth that we're going to discuss further on in this talk.

37
00:04:26,194 --> 00:04:33,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if such... if such a reasoning would have followed, then nobody would have ever listened to the Buddha.

38
00:04:34,022 --> 00:04:42,430
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha was literally the only one in the entire cosmos saying certain things, teaching the dhamma.

39
00:04:42,470 --> 00:04:44,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody else can teach the dhamma except the Buddha.

40
00:04:45,232 --> 00:04:50,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if people were to go by the criteria of majority is always right,

41
00:04:50,594 --> 00:04:53,819
[SPEAKER_00]: dhamma would have not arisen ever.

42
00:04:54,199 --> 00:05:01,791
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's kind of a contradictory standpoint for somebody who wants to practice the dhamma today.

43
00:05:02,494 --> 00:05:10,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, dismisses the instructions because they're not part of the established majority or it's, you know, I just heard it once and so on.

44
00:05:10,718 --> 00:05:15,140
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it doesn't matter if you just hear it briefly from one person and never ever again from anybody else.

45
00:05:15,801 --> 00:05:28,947
[SPEAKER_00]: It still requires... it still deserves the equal amount of consideration and sort of contrasting against the principle of greed, aversion, delusion, non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

46
00:05:30,152 --> 00:05:33,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And then on the basis of that, you can decide if it's right or wrong.

47
00:05:33,973 --> 00:05:36,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on the basis of how many people agree with it or not.

48
00:05:38,494 --> 00:05:43,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if that thing is the truth, it's a mistake to take it simply on the basis of repeated hearing.

49
00:05:44,635 --> 00:05:46,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the majority said so, that's it.

50
00:05:46,296 --> 00:05:47,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Who am I to disagree?

51
00:05:47,996 --> 00:05:49,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what Buddha says here.

52
00:05:49,376 --> 00:05:55,858
[SPEAKER_00]: You should be the one who will then question that, despite a vast majority saying that that is so.

53
00:05:56,618 --> 00:06:04,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you question it by the principles that he outlines in the same sutta, which is, these things are good and these things are bad.

54
00:06:04,661 --> 00:06:12,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that are good are things that are lust-free, greed-free, ill-will-free, distraction-free.

55
00:06:12,384 --> 00:06:15,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Things, basically, that are not avoiding responsibility, but taking up responsibility.

56
00:06:16,755 --> 00:06:23,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that are bad, are things that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, and everything else, desire, sensuality, everything else that revolves around them.

57
00:06:24,376 --> 00:06:35,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, majority might be saying something, and whether that's right or wrong, you can reflect on the basis of the principle of greed, non-greed, aversion, non-aversion, delusion, and so on.

58
00:06:36,418 --> 00:06:45,660
[SPEAKER_00]: If, for example, majority says, no, no, no, like household life is good, and practice of dhamma is not so good, or it's not necessary to become a monk, and so on.

59
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:58,627
[SPEAKER_00]: You ask yourself, okay, so the whole world might be agreeing upon this, but which one of those two ways, one claim is right, the other one claim is wrong, leads to dispassion or passion?

60
00:06:59,107 --> 00:07:03,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Leads to greed, aversion, delusion or freedom from greed, aversion, delusion?

61
00:07:03,489 --> 00:07:09,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one leads to liberation from acquisitions, from ownerships, from burden?

62
00:07:09,653 --> 00:07:11,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And which one leads to accumulation of those?

63
00:07:12,341 --> 00:07:16,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So you use that now as a criteria to see whether the whole majority is right or wrong.

64
00:07:17,292 --> 00:07:20,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Not simply, well, everybody said so, thus it must be right.

65
00:07:21,251 --> 00:07:25,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the similar... the one that follows is that don't go simply upon tradition.

66
00:07:25,434 --> 00:07:26,115
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very similar.

67
00:07:26,155 --> 00:07:29,938
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just like a more... more specific type of majority.

68
00:07:30,158 --> 00:07:31,740
[SPEAKER_00]: The majority of my tradition.

69
00:07:32,020 --> 00:07:44,250
[SPEAKER_00]: And you often encounter that in the in the monastic settings, whereby, like, you know, the monks that themselves would be quoting Kalama Sutta, at the same time, refuse to simply put tradition in question, or the traditional ways.

70
00:07:44,750 --> 00:07:48,633
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not for the sake of dismantling tradition or fighting against it.

71
00:07:48,673 --> 00:07:54,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It's simply for the sake of not using that as a sole criteria, because that's not where greed, aversion, delusion are.

72
00:07:54,977 --> 00:08:07,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your goal is to practice the dhamma for the liberation of your mind, none of these external criterias can be taken as trustworthy, because greed, aversion, delusion are on the level of your own intention, your own mind, your own choices.

73
00:08:08,505 --> 00:08:16,067
[SPEAKER_00]: And that can only be visible to you if you have stopped resorting to these external criterias of self-justification.

74
00:08:16,127 --> 00:08:21,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, well, I don't think about it much because that's just how we do it in this tradition.

75
00:08:21,949 --> 00:08:25,870
[SPEAKER_00]: And to make things worse today, you know, having been so long since the Buddha passed away,

76
00:08:27,059 --> 00:08:31,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Often you find Buddhist traditions that are completely the opposite of what the Buddha said you should do.

77
00:08:32,163 --> 00:08:33,203
[SPEAKER_00]: And people would know that.

78
00:08:33,323 --> 00:08:40,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in full awareness of the Buddha said one thing, tradition says another thing, but I go with the tradition because, you know, it's been done for so long.

79
00:08:40,988 --> 00:08:45,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, because the majority of the people that I align myself with agree with this.

80
00:08:46,392 --> 00:08:49,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, on a more personal level, out of... out of fear.

81
00:08:50,075 --> 00:08:53,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of a fear to... to stand out.

82
00:08:54,197 --> 00:08:56,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So, although I know what the Buddha said,

83
00:08:56,946 --> 00:08:58,807
[SPEAKER_00]: The tradition requires me to do something else.

84
00:08:59,007 --> 00:09:02,068
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just do something else, so nobody will criticize me or blame me.

85
00:09:02,868 --> 00:09:06,089
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you should really re-evaluate why do you want to practice dhamma in the first place.

86
00:09:06,309 --> 00:09:10,511
[SPEAKER_00]: To soothe the majority or to free yourself from saṃsara once and for all.

87
00:09:11,427 --> 00:09:18,153
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that results in avoidance of personal responsibility, and if there is no taking up of personal responsibility, there is no practice for dhamma.

88
00:09:19,094 --> 00:09:28,642
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, even if the tradition is right, you mustn't take it on the level of tradition, you must take it on the level of the personal responsibility for greed, aversion, delusion, or freedom from those.

89
00:09:29,463 --> 00:09:38,525
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point of not going by mere tradition, it's not, you know, anarchy and sort of rebellion against it.

90
00:09:38,945 --> 00:09:44,247
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, that's not... if you are going by it, you are avoiding responsibility.

91
00:09:44,467 --> 00:09:45,667
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to take it up.

92
00:09:46,007 --> 00:09:49,868
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you choose to follow tradition, you choose to because you made the choice to do so.

93
00:09:50,048 --> 00:09:50,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's on you.

94
00:09:51,569 --> 00:09:56,170
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you choose to do something that tradition says you should do, you do it because you choose to do it.

95
00:09:57,587 --> 00:10:01,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because tradition said so, and I'm absolved from responsibility for doing so.

96
00:10:01,790 --> 00:10:14,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it always comes back on you, and the sooner you step away from that group belonging of the tradition, or majority, or we are in this together, the sooner your practice will start.

97
00:10:15,701 --> 00:10:18,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the third one, don't go upon hearsay.

98
00:10:18,703 --> 00:10:23,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So, itikīra, the Pali word, and it's the whole point is the hearsay.

99
00:10:24,007 --> 00:10:25,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Just, you heard something,

100
00:10:26,927 --> 00:10:27,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Seems right and that's it.

101
00:10:28,149 --> 00:10:31,613
[SPEAKER_00]: That's enough on its own to kind of accept that argument.

102
00:10:32,053 --> 00:10:35,698
[SPEAKER_00]: That's even more careless than the previous two examples.

103
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:40,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, anecdotal evidence would come under this as well.

104
00:10:40,644 --> 00:10:41,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Rumor and so on.

105
00:10:41,906 --> 00:10:42,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like...

106
00:10:44,323 --> 00:10:46,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, such-and-such Ajahn said so.

107
00:10:46,466 --> 00:10:47,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Something happened to him.

108
00:10:47,547 --> 00:10:51,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, that's the truth that applies to all of us.

109
00:10:51,312 --> 00:10:56,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, such-and-such person interprets certain rules or practices in this way.

110
00:10:56,459 --> 00:11:00,825
[SPEAKER_00]: I heard that from somebody else, but that's good enough for me to follow it.

111
00:11:01,450 --> 00:11:02,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't go upon what's in the scripture.

112
00:11:03,271 --> 00:11:04,532
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's an interesting one.

113
00:11:04,732 --> 00:11:12,036
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, the authority of the texts, and you find that same even in the early Buddhist, you know, suttas.

114
00:11:12,757 --> 00:11:15,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in them, but in people's attitude towards them.

115
00:11:16,039 --> 00:11:16,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, eh...

116
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:20,121
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how authoritative text might be.

117
00:11:20,141 --> 00:11:25,645
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, the entirety of it, it's still on the level of what the Buddha called phrasing.

118
00:11:26,285 --> 00:11:30,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is phrasing of something, and then there is meaning of what you just put in phrases.

119
00:11:31,577 --> 00:11:42,899
[SPEAKER_00]: And scriptures are the phrasing, which means every single individual that chooses to read the scriptures is responsible for the meaning they infer from those phrases.

120
00:11:44,619 --> 00:11:51,640
[SPEAKER_00]: And that also applies even to the individual terms and designations in the individual words.

121
00:11:51,680 --> 00:12:00,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when you're just doing a mere translation, you're already coming with certain preconceived notions of the meaning behind those terms.

122
00:12:01,157 --> 00:12:15,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, when you compile some broader meaning on the basis of a broader collection of phrases, all of those individual little meanings that you have inferred, do kind of influence the general understanding that you got from it.

123
00:12:15,601 --> 00:12:21,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why that taking up responsibility from... on the smallest level, it's really important.

124
00:12:22,042 --> 00:12:24,123
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why even if it's the Buddha's words,

125
00:12:25,638 --> 00:12:29,882
[SPEAKER_00]: written down accurately in the suttas and we all agree this is what he said.

126
00:12:30,502 --> 00:12:31,944
[SPEAKER_00]: That's still not an objective truth.

127
00:12:33,024 --> 00:12:37,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Objective truth is the assembly of those phrases, the arrangement.

128
00:12:37,328 --> 00:12:38,629
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the objectivity of it.

129
00:12:38,829 --> 00:12:42,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning from it can never be placed on the scripture itself.

130
00:12:43,455 --> 00:12:45,518
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what we said in the other talks.

131
00:12:46,098 --> 00:12:55,750
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole point of practice, of studying and trying to understand the Buddha's phrases, instructions, designations and so on, is to be free from greed, aversion, delusion.

132
00:12:56,031 --> 00:12:57,172
[SPEAKER_00]: To be free from passion.

133
00:12:58,233 --> 00:12:58,514
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

134
00:13:00,701 --> 00:13:07,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Study of phrases, if it's not resulting in freedom from greed, aversion, delusion, you are not understanding its meaning.

135
00:13:07,744 --> 00:13:17,567
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how well skilled and informed and and and scholarly trained and understanding every single etymology and permutation and declinations of the word are.

136
00:13:18,268 --> 00:13:24,190
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are not, at least, sotāpanna, free from greed, aversion, delusion, practice, intuition, none of these phrases apply to you.

137
00:13:24,910 --> 00:13:26,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the phrases say themselves.

138
00:13:27,531 --> 00:13:31,053
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody were to understand the Buddha's instruction, they would have at least the right view.

139
00:13:31,914 --> 00:13:42,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this particular example of don't go upon what's in the scripture, it's quite relevant for most of us, because you do have to start somewhere.

140
00:13:42,179 --> 00:13:48,183
[SPEAKER_00]: You do have to start from some sort of faith, in some sort of authority that can tell you how to practice.

141
00:13:48,983 --> 00:14:01,191
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... it's easy then to fall into a view that that's the truth in itself and that you are not responsible for how you're already approaching it with your attitudes and preconceived notions and implied meanings and so on.

142
00:14:01,752 --> 00:14:11,158
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you need the right phrasing, but, as the other requisite for the right view, is you also need to have... you also need to know how to attend things properly.

143
00:14:11,398 --> 00:14:13,100
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much what this comes down to.

144
00:14:14,390 --> 00:14:21,491
[SPEAKER_00]: If you attend things properly, you will know that correct understanding of the meaning of the phrasing has to result in dispassion.

145
00:14:22,111 --> 00:14:29,993
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no dispassion, there is no disenchantment, there is no relinquishment, there is no right view, you are not understanding the meaning of the phrasing.

146
00:14:30,033 --> 00:14:41,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how satisfied and how much clarity you might have on account of your, you know, informational collection of all the phrases and that you have memorized and know by heart and so on.

147
00:14:42,252 --> 00:14:58,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't put the responsibility for the meaning of what you're trying to understand on the level of scholastic studies of the phrases, because that's not where the meaning is, and by putting it there, you're pretty much, again, comes back to the same, avoiding responsibility,

148
00:14:59,788 --> 00:15:02,889
[SPEAKER_00]: For your own interpretation of the meaning that, again, it's not optional.

149
00:15:03,269 --> 00:15:14,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're approaching just a brand new text that you've never seen before, you're already approaching it with preconceived notions, preconceived meanings of the terms that appear through those phrases and so on.

150
00:15:16,275 --> 00:15:26,158
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I said in some other talk, like, you don't really... Again, if you have self-transparency, take up responsibility, you don't even need to be

151
00:15:27,946 --> 00:15:35,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Going too far into the study of the phrases, you can even use your own language and just say consciousness.

152
00:15:35,331 --> 00:15:38,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, Pali is viññāṇa, means consciousness.

153
00:15:38,333 --> 00:15:41,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so now you have two phrases that you just compared with each other.

154
00:15:42,376 --> 00:15:44,318
[SPEAKER_00]: One phrase is in Pali, one phrase is in English.

155
00:15:44,878 --> 00:15:46,759
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the meaning of either of those?

156
00:15:46,799 --> 00:15:50,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Or what is the meaning of that phenomenon that you're designating through these phrases?

157
00:15:50,740 --> 00:15:57,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you will not arrive at that meaning if you start studying the etymology and the root of the viññāṇa and how it came to be in consciousness and so on.

158
00:15:57,363 --> 00:15:59,464
[SPEAKER_00]: That might enrich your information.

159
00:15:59,484 --> 00:16:01,145
[SPEAKER_00]: That can help you look for the meaning.

160
00:16:01,385 --> 00:16:05,907
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if your attitude is not to look for the meaning, but onto the authority of the texts,

161
00:16:06,688 --> 00:16:09,590
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole domain of where the meaning is, is still invisible to you.

162
00:16:10,250 --> 00:16:25,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of going into more detail, more often than not, what you need to do is go back onto the word that you already know, consciousness, in your own language, whatever it is, and say, okay, but when I say consciousness in my own language, what am I designating?

163
00:16:26,299 --> 00:16:27,900
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the meaning of that phrase?

164
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:31,882
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it, in my experience, that I call consciousness when I say it?

165
00:16:32,522 --> 00:16:38,531
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you will realize that vast majority of your own day-to-day terms and phrases that you use are not understood.

166
00:16:38,651 --> 00:16:44,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they were, you would pretty much be understanding the basis, the framework of your experience as such.

167
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:48,225
[SPEAKER_00]: You would be developing clarity in regard to the five aggregates.

168
00:16:49,270 --> 00:16:58,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not going upon what's in the scripture, means not dismissing the scriptures and never reading the suttas, means taking up responsibility for what those scriptures mean to you.

169
00:16:59,415 --> 00:17:00,095
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

170
00:17:00,676 --> 00:17:05,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Just reading a sutta right away would result in you liberating your mind from dispassion.

171
00:17:05,358 --> 00:17:13,182
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't, because of all the preconceived notions about the terms and phrases that you are reading, that you already carry, all those notions.

172
00:17:13,502 --> 00:17:17,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Translating one phrase to the other does not highlight the meaning.

173
00:17:17,766 --> 00:17:23,412
[SPEAKER_00]: It can bring you closer if you know where to look for it, but in itself, it's just comparison between different phrases.

174
00:17:23,532 --> 00:17:24,913
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

175
00:17:25,234 --> 00:17:36,925
[SPEAKER_00]: In itself, scholarly study, scholastic study, cannot result in any revelation of the meaning, because for that, taking up that personal responsibility that precedes your scholastic study,

176
00:17:37,726 --> 00:17:57,916
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your study is then guided by the right attention, correctly attending to things, only for the sake of clarifying meaning, not for the sake of satisfying intellectual craving for the, you know, more detailed study of these phrases and comparison and its development through history and so on.

177
00:17:58,856 --> 00:18:00,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, all pretty much becomes a distraction.

178
00:18:02,360 --> 00:18:07,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't go upon something that may be true, even if there is no evidence to confirm it.

179
00:18:08,742 --> 00:18:16,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, in itself, thing might be true, but that in itself is not enough to accept that as a valid premise.

180
00:18:17,525 --> 00:18:25,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, dispassion might be true, but now I can say, yes, dispassion is true, nibbāna is true, that's it.

181
00:18:25,507 --> 00:18:26,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna is real.

182
00:18:26,928 --> 00:18:27,548
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done my work.

183
00:18:27,608 --> 00:18:28,808
[SPEAKER_00]: I accepted that as a truth.

184
00:18:28,908 --> 00:18:29,028
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

185
00:18:29,428 --> 00:18:31,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna is real only if you attain it.

186
00:18:32,451 --> 00:18:35,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a possibility of nibbāna being real for you.

187
00:18:35,132 --> 00:18:38,494
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha meant by element of nibbāna present in everyone's experience.

188
00:18:39,034 --> 00:18:41,015
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can see how that happened in later schools.

189
00:18:41,615 --> 00:18:43,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply, it may be true.

190
00:18:43,736 --> 00:18:46,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it is real as such, objective truth.

191
00:18:46,198 --> 00:18:48,439
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then people started saying, we are already nibbāna.

192
00:18:49,383 --> 00:18:54,448
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to do anything because nibbāna has a possibility of something that I can develop.

193
00:18:54,468 --> 00:18:55,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, as such, it's true.

194
00:18:55,569 --> 00:18:56,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I'm already in it.

195
00:18:57,591 --> 00:19:00,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, it needs to be verified for yourself.

196
00:19:00,554 --> 00:19:05,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, if the phrases are understood correctly, they will result in this passion.

197
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:09,883
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if nibbāna is understood correctly, you will be in it.

198
00:19:10,588 --> 00:19:18,051
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just say, yes, I believe in nibbāna, I accept it as a true objective value of this reality, but I'm not in it.

199
00:19:18,231 --> 00:19:19,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it doesn't apply to you.

200
00:19:19,431 --> 00:19:21,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there is no nibbāna for you.

201
00:19:22,513 --> 00:19:33,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the next one is, basically, don't go upon an axiom, which, basically, nāyahetu means the first, the first cause, the first premise.

202
00:19:33,917 --> 00:19:35,218
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, don't go upon the

203
00:19:36,295 --> 00:19:42,897
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no such as the... because this premise is first, because somebody said it first, to that extent, that must not be questioned.

204
00:19:42,977 --> 00:19:46,539
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything else that come after it, you can question that, but not the first premise.

205
00:19:46,979 --> 00:19:53,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think, probably, the easiest example is the... like the... generally, the Christian view of belief in God.

206
00:19:54,361 --> 00:19:59,043
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can question everything, even in like a... you know, there was like existential philosophers who were Christians.

207
00:19:59,543 --> 00:20:03,785
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would question everything in the entire experience, but they wouldn't question the premise that the God is real as such.

208
00:20:04,972 --> 00:20:07,333
[SPEAKER_00]: And that in itself is is a contradiction.

209
00:20:07,413 --> 00:20:12,274
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you question everything, you have to question even your starting premise that made you question everything.

210
00:20:12,815 --> 00:20:20,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nothing should be exempted from turning your reasoning to it and applying these same principles of greed, aversion, delusion, freedom from greed, aversion, delusion.

211
00:20:21,976 --> 00:20:23,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't go upon faulty reasoning.

212
00:20:23,798 --> 00:20:24,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of self-explanatory.

213
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:42,880
[SPEAKER_00]: This one, it would also fall like common sense, or using a personal experience as a argument to apply to these universal truths that, you know, the dhamma is supposed to reveal, greed, aversion, delusion and freedom from it.

214
00:20:43,573 --> 00:20:47,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, it would be for somebody who is not free from sensuality.

215
00:20:48,658 --> 00:20:54,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, actually, there was an example in the suttas of that, where he met a monk who said, oh, the greatest happiness is when you're free from sensuality.

216
00:20:55,103 --> 00:20:58,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, this person, not being free from sensuality himself,

217
00:21:00,909 --> 00:21:07,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he said, no, I don't see that.

218
00:21:07,596 --> 00:21:08,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, that's not true.

219
00:21:09,918 --> 00:21:11,159
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a fallacy in itself.

220
00:21:11,219 --> 00:21:16,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you might not see it, but that doesn't mean that you wouldn't see it if you make the right effort.

221
00:21:17,045 --> 00:21:21,449
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't see the truth personally, it doesn't mean there is no such truth.

222
00:21:22,109 --> 00:21:25,913
[SPEAKER_00]: And the simile the Buddha gave, it's like the two friends come to the bottom of the mountain,

223
00:21:26,747 --> 00:21:35,623
[SPEAKER_00]: One of them decides to climb the top, and when he's at the top, he exclaims, wow, the views from this top of this mountain is just, like, so beautiful, you can't even describe them.

224
00:21:36,224 --> 00:21:37,847
[SPEAKER_00]: The man at the bottom of the mountain says,

225
00:21:38,268 --> 00:21:44,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Turns around, he doesn't see anything, just some forest, and he says, no, I don't believe that's the truth, because I don't see it for myself.

226
00:21:45,749 --> 00:21:50,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the man from the top comes, takes him by the arm, drags him up to the top of the mountain and says, do you see now?

227
00:21:50,590 --> 00:21:51,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he agreed to, yes.

228
00:21:52,231 --> 00:21:53,771
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's it's it's it's the same.

229
00:21:53,811 --> 00:22:01,933
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody wants to see the benefit of freedom from sensuality, they cannot see it before they're free from sensuality.

230
00:22:03,783 --> 00:22:10,229
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's even worse then, if they cannot see it, before they're free from sensuality, and then they choose to dismiss it as well, because they can't see it.

231
00:22:11,190 --> 00:22:15,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they will never decide to climb that hill and see the benefit for themselves.

232
00:22:15,674 --> 00:22:17,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't go upon bias.

233
00:22:18,537 --> 00:22:21,199
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's again, everybody starts with the bias.

234
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:26,484
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but, don't go upon bias means start questioning

235
00:22:27,418 --> 00:22:32,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting yourself in question, that even when you're absolutely certain, you might be biased.

236
00:22:32,741 --> 00:22:40,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't know for sure, what the right view is, what freedom from greed, aversion, delusion is, means you are biased, whether you see it or not.

237
00:22:41,127 --> 00:22:55,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it will be safer for you, in terms of the authentic, eh... contradiction-free type of reflection, that can result in the right view, it will be safer for you to regard yourself as already biased, even if you don't think you are.

238
00:22:56,953 --> 00:23:01,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you truly are not biased, putting yourself in doubt will not affect you.

239
00:23:01,475 --> 00:23:04,417
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why sotāpanna is beyond doubt.

240
00:23:04,917 --> 00:23:06,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't go upon another's apparent competence.

241
00:23:06,998 --> 00:23:15,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's if somebody is just, like, again, a confident speaker, seems to know everything, seems to have an answer to everything.

242
00:23:16,823 --> 00:23:32,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if he might be the Buddha himself, talking the absolute truth, if you choose to regard things that he's saying as an objective truth, because the manner of the way he's saying them, because of his competence about it, that still does not absolve you from not seeing those things for yourself.

243
00:23:32,696 --> 00:23:33,637
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, for yourself.

244
00:23:34,037 --> 00:23:40,983
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, even the ultimate truth that the Buddha teaches, unless you have the right view, is not your truth, does not apply to you.

245
00:23:42,198 --> 00:23:54,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the apparent competence of another cannot be used as an objective truth, objective value, that you are partaking in, unless you saw it for yourself, in which case you wouldn't need competence of another.

246
00:23:54,582 --> 00:23:55,822
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't need majority.

247
00:23:56,643 --> 00:23:58,423
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't need tradition to support you.

248
00:23:58,943 --> 00:24:00,724
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't need people to agree with you.

249
00:24:00,864 --> 00:24:07,146
[SPEAKER_00]: There's another another sutta where the man said, who has developed the right view, he says, even if the whole world says the Buddha is wrong,

250
00:24:09,110 --> 00:24:11,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, he cannot be swayed by any of these external

251
00:24:22,185 --> 00:24:27,108
[SPEAKER_00]: standpoints, because he sees the core, the root of the problem, and he sees the way out.

252
00:24:27,328 --> 00:24:48,978
[SPEAKER_00]: And the root of the problem is greed, aversion, delusion, that affects your thinking, affects your bias, affects your perception, your reasoning, and makes you avoid the responsibility for pretty much already implying meaning in phrasing and in scriptures and tradition and everything else, but at the same time claiming, no, it's because of the tradition, it's because of the scriptures, not because of how I took it to mean.

253
00:24:49,914 --> 00:25:08,305
[SPEAKER_00]: And the point to make here is that this can sound like some sort of you're just determined, or you're like a sort of a fundamentalist decided upon his religion, his interpretation, that it's right and the whole world is wrong.

254
00:25:08,885 --> 00:25:09,185
[SPEAKER_00]: But no.

255
00:25:09,925 --> 00:25:17,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Sotāpanna, in this case, has arrived at that, such confidence, not through deciding and through following these logical fallacies,

256
00:25:18,954 --> 00:25:35,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that means that the entire majority that disagrees with him, or disagrees with the Buddha, if they were to free their mind from greed, aversion, delusion, they will see the same thing.

257
00:25:37,434 --> 00:25:41,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the end, they would agree because they all climbed the top of that mountain.

258
00:25:41,515 --> 00:25:47,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why even the Buddha himself said he didn't invent the dhamma or create the dhamma and so on.

259
00:25:47,837 --> 00:25:49,297
[SPEAKER_00]: He just rediscovered it.

260
00:25:49,357 --> 00:25:54,159
[SPEAKER_00]: He just found it like an ancient path that has been overgrown by the jungle.

261
00:25:54,219 --> 00:25:55,419
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he just uncovered it.

262
00:25:56,779 --> 00:26:13,965
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it goes against the preconceived notions and greed, aversion, delusion of the entire world, but it also allows the possibility for the entire world, if they were to free themselves from those preconceived notions, greed, aversion, delusion, conceit and so on, to see and walk the same path.

263
00:26:15,849 --> 00:26:19,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then the last one is, yeah, don't go simply upon consideration.

264
00:26:19,731 --> 00:26:25,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he's our teacher and thus, thus, we'll just listen to what he says and I won't have to think for myself.

265
00:26:26,436 --> 00:26:27,837
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, and that that is quite common.

266
00:26:28,257 --> 00:26:39,626
[SPEAKER_00]: People tend to get off on to the, off on the attitude of, you know, a big famous established teacher or confident speaker that arouses energy in them and wants to make them practice.

267
00:26:39,666 --> 00:26:42,828
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, unless you are taking up responsibility for that,

268
00:26:43,958 --> 00:26:55,847
[SPEAKER_00]: experience of a confident teacher, you will be pretty much projecting and putting the objective value of practice onto that teacher, as if it is you're doing it, although none of that applies to you.

269
00:26:56,868 --> 00:27:02,773
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a monk might be your teacher, but that still doesn't mean that he can do the work for you.

270
00:27:04,174 --> 00:27:07,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, that's why it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a logical contradiction.

271
00:27:08,537 --> 00:27:10,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it goes further to simply encourage karmas to

272
00:27:13,470 --> 00:27:25,949
[SPEAKER_00]: To start using these more universal criteria, for which they are personally responsible, such as greed, aversion, delusion, desire, freedom from desire, and so on, to infer whether what being said aligns.

273
00:27:26,959 --> 00:27:34,922
[SPEAKER_00]: With those universal criteria, they would result in general wholesomeness, or unwholesomeness, as the case might be.

274
00:27:35,522 --> 00:27:38,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody might ask, yeah, but why is greed bad and why is aversion?

275
00:27:38,383 --> 00:27:40,703
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, that's something you can arrive for yourself.

276
00:27:40,743 --> 00:27:43,984
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole world can say, you know, greed is not bad.

277
00:27:44,304 --> 00:27:55,368
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you stop and think about it, clearly, rationally, not avoiding responsibility for it, you will arrive at certain universal conclusions that, yeah, whenever there is a bad thing done in the world,

278
00:27:56,632 --> 00:28:02,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Adultery, cheating, killing, stealing, lying, harm, disrespect, disregard.

279
00:28:03,318 --> 00:28:10,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Can any of those things be done if there is no greed, lust, aversion, passion involved?

280
00:28:11,885 --> 00:28:14,608
[SPEAKER_00]: If a mind is free from greed, can it harm another?

281
00:28:15,268 --> 00:28:17,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Can it want what somebody else has?

282
00:28:18,442 --> 00:28:27,464
[SPEAKER_00]: If a mind is not free from greed, my own mind, can it experience the pressure of desires that basically start controlling you and your actions and your choices and you lose perspective?

283
00:28:27,704 --> 00:28:28,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

284
00:28:28,425 --> 00:28:42,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, universally, whenever something unwholesome, unskillful happens to you or to others or both, it has to be either on the basis of greed, aversion or complete careless distraction and avoidance of responsibility.

285
00:28:43,042 --> 00:28:44,286
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why they are bad.

286
00:28:44,707 --> 00:28:49,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, sometimes good things might be done out of greed or aversion, but that is secondary.

287
00:28:49,826 --> 00:28:58,149
[SPEAKER_00]: The root of the problem, the framework, because of which the whole mass of suffering and fighting and conflict and killing exists in the world, remains unchanged.

288
00:28:58,549 --> 00:29:05,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it doesn't always result in killing or lying or stealing, but potential for it remains and sooner or later it will.

289
00:29:06,111 --> 00:29:12,493
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's better off, then, to not have such basis existing in your own mind, basis of greed, aversion, delusion.

290
00:29:13,293 --> 00:29:15,859
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, whatever you do will be good.

291
00:29:16,019 --> 00:29:18,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you do will be selfless.

292
00:29:18,866 --> 00:29:22,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you do will be free from ill will and friendly.

293
00:29:23,545 --> 00:29:31,667
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, greed, aversion, delusion are completely redundant for good, for doing good, but are absolutely necessary when the bad is done.

294
00:29:32,627 --> 00:29:35,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's better to abandon it.

295
00:29:35,528 --> 00:29:36,448
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better to avoid it.

296
00:29:36,508 --> 00:29:37,509
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better to give it up.

297
00:29:38,389 --> 00:29:49,172
[SPEAKER_00]: So, using those criteria, then, when you follow, when you hear different philosophies, different teachers, different instructions, you ask yourself, okay, so, does this lead to freedom from greed, aversion, delusion?

298
00:29:49,752 --> 00:29:55,737
[SPEAKER_00]: to what extent, in what manner, or it doesn't even deal with it.

299
00:29:56,098 --> 00:30:07,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you realize that it doesn't even address those things, and it certainly does not help mind not free from greed, aversion, delusion, to reach freedom from greed, aversion, delusion, then it's not really a relevant teaching.

300
00:30:08,189 --> 00:30:10,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

301
00:30:11,271 --> 00:30:11,912
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

302
00:30:13,569 --> 00:30:20,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then the Buddha goes saying that, says, does the greed appear in a man's mind for his benefit or harm?

303
00:30:21,394 --> 00:30:34,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Being given to greed, being overwhelmed and conquered by mentally overwhelmed by the pressure of greed, the man takes life, steals, commits adultery, tells lies, prompts another to do so, and so on.

304
00:30:34,962 --> 00:30:36,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what we just said.

305
00:30:36,503 --> 00:30:39,805
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's same for greed, aversion and delusion.

306
00:30:40,627 --> 00:30:44,469
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you shouldn't go by these external circumstances.

307
00:30:44,549 --> 00:30:45,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, external criteria.

308
00:30:46,930 --> 00:30:50,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Majority, tradition, hearsay, authority of another, and so on.

309
00:30:50,632 --> 00:30:57,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they mask your responsibility for the meaning behind those criteria.

310
00:30:58,255 --> 00:31:00,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning for which you are responsible.

311
00:31:00,596 --> 00:31:05,619
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as your responsibility for the meaning is covered up, greed, aversion, delusion are invisible.

312
00:31:06,547 --> 00:31:11,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you don't really see what's beneficial for you and what isn't.

313
00:31:12,869 --> 00:31:19,452
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, first, you don't go by these criteria on their own, but you question, reflect, put them in doubt, pretty much.

314
00:31:20,455 --> 00:31:29,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the responsibility for your choices, for your actions, and for the meaning that you get from things, will be felt as yours.

315
00:31:30,544 --> 00:31:31,805
[SPEAKER_00]: You will feel the weight of it.

316
00:31:32,066 --> 00:31:33,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I could do this, or I could do that.

317
00:31:34,468 --> 00:31:37,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, it's on me, which means the consequences of that will be equally on me.

318
00:31:37,791 --> 00:31:41,574
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, it's easier if I say, I'll just do that because our tradition does this.

319
00:31:42,315 --> 00:31:46,096
[SPEAKER_00]: See, now, whatever comes out of it, I won't feel responsible for it.

320
00:31:46,376 --> 00:31:48,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what that results in.

321
00:31:48,157 --> 00:31:49,657
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll say, well, I just followed tradition.

322
00:31:49,677 --> 00:31:50,697
[SPEAKER_00]: I just did my duty.

323
00:31:51,297 --> 00:31:54,338
[SPEAKER_00]: And you hear that a lot, like, among, like, soldiers who are in the army.

324
00:31:54,758 --> 00:31:57,939
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, they actually go and kill people, saying, oh, it's my duty.

325
00:31:58,119 --> 00:32:01,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but you chose to take on that duty.

326
00:32:02,180 --> 00:32:05,061
[SPEAKER_00]: You chose the meaning of that duty for you.

327
00:32:05,321 --> 00:32:08,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, all the actions that come out of that duty are on you.

328
00:32:09,693 --> 00:32:10,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

329
00:32:10,575 --> 00:32:13,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if the entire society agrees that it's a good and wholesome thing to do.

330
00:32:13,923 --> 00:32:15,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Protect your country and so on.

331
00:32:15,606 --> 00:32:16,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're engaging in killing...

332
00:32:18,895 --> 00:32:33,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Which cannot be done without greed, without aversion, without some sort of delusion, which means you are engaging in unwholesome states, that will have unwholesome results, yet you're fully oblivious, blind, cover up the responsibility for it, because you projected it onto the duty.

333
00:32:34,084 --> 00:32:35,505
[SPEAKER_00]: See, this duty is impersonal.

334
00:32:35,625 --> 00:32:37,606
[SPEAKER_00]: I just serve my country, or something like that.

335
00:32:38,367 --> 00:32:41,849
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you serve your own interpretation of the meaning of serving the country.

336
00:32:42,409 --> 00:32:43,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you serve yourself.

337
00:32:44,010 --> 00:32:45,131
[SPEAKER_00]: You're always responsible.

338
00:32:46,220 --> 00:33:05,554
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not a pleasant truth for vast majority of people, because vast majority of people live by placing their responsibility for their actions, for their interpretations, for their meaning onto the external authority, onto the external criteria, onto the, eh... onto the external, you know, views, religion, philosophy, tradition, and so on.

339
00:33:05,714 --> 00:33:11,418
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why they do remain victims of their own greed, aversion, delusion, and then subjected to everything else that follows.

340
00:33:11,998 --> 00:33:18,583
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also why, for example, for somebody who has seen the right view, he says, nobody can be my guide.

341
00:33:18,883 --> 00:33:29,992
[SPEAKER_00]: No external circumstance can be taken as my guide to tell me what to do, because internally, I see what needs to be done, universally, to be free from greed, aversion, delusion.

342
00:33:30,692 --> 00:33:52,049
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why also, no amount of that kind of the majority, the blame, the worldly whims that Buddha talked about, praise, blame, fame, disrepute, all of that, none of that can really existentially affect or disturb somebody with the right view.

343
00:33:52,890 --> 00:33:59,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he doesn't depend anymore on those external criteria for avoidance of his own responsibility.

344
00:34:00,033 --> 00:34:18,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, when that external, for example, majority or tradition, if they're against something that the individual sotāpanna might be doing, he won't be affected by it in the same manner he would be affected by it if he is bound by that tradition or by majority for avoidance of his personal responsibility.

345
00:34:18,642 --> 00:34:26,667
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why praise and blame will cease to affect you, because you don't exist anymore in that publicly assumed domain where praise and blame apply.

346
00:34:26,707 --> 00:34:35,852
[SPEAKER_00]: And you existed there before, because you used that assumed domain to avoid responsibility for your own actions, choices, interpretation and meaning.

347
00:34:36,292 --> 00:34:45,738
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are not affected by praise and blame, because you don't existentially depend on that assumed domain anymore, you will not have ill will towards people either.

348
00:34:46,813 --> 00:34:48,556
[SPEAKER_00]: People that criticize you, disagree with you.

349
00:34:49,518 --> 00:34:53,345
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean you pretend that, you know, what they say is right or something like that.

350
00:34:53,385 --> 00:34:59,076
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you have the objective, more objective criteria now, the criteria of the right view, but...

351
00:34:59,995 --> 00:35:13,465
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of criticism, no amount of attack by majority or hearsay, none of those reasons can be taken personally now, because you're not personally involved with that assumed domain anymore.

352
00:35:14,346 --> 00:35:27,856
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, if somebody does cultivate the criteria that the Buddha instructed Kalamas to do, yeah, they will go beyond ill-will in as much as they go beyond passion, because it kind of goes hand in hand.

353
00:35:28,036 --> 00:35:28,837
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pretty obvious.

354
00:35:29,767 --> 00:35:44,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because you're not out there, publicly assumed, on the level of traditional majority, thus, when that traditional majority might disagree or attack you or criticize you, you will not be able to take it personally, because you're not personally involved.

355
00:35:46,368 --> 00:35:59,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, which means you won't lose perspective, which means if, say, majority starts disagreeing with something that, you know, a sotāpanna does, he won't act out of fear, because he's not afraid of them, but it doesn't mean he'll dismiss what they're saying either.

356
00:35:59,891 --> 00:36:04,172
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll have perspective, he'll have enough space to see, well, maybe they do have a point.

357
00:36:04,292 --> 00:36:06,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that they understand the meaning behind it, but that doesn't matter.

358
00:36:08,953 --> 00:36:18,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you wouldn't have a problem changing the ways, or not changing the ways, depending on, you know, if greed, aversion, delusion would be sort of encouraged by changing the ways.

359
00:36:19,500 --> 00:36:31,669
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when the suttas, you know, they're like many of those in the Vinaya, especially for the monks, there are many like smaller rules, kind of like circumstantially arisen responses to the environment, to people at the time, you know.

360
00:36:32,109 --> 00:36:37,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Monks lived in certain areas where people didn't want them to, I don't know, walk across the fields during this time of year.

361
00:36:38,391 --> 00:36:42,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they complain that we said don't walk across the field during that time of year, and so on.

362
00:36:42,273 --> 00:36:52,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, none of those... none of those really... none of those rules, none of those standpoints are kind of directly relevant to its practice.

363
00:36:53,457 --> 00:36:58,439
[SPEAKER_00]: But what is relevant to the practice is being left alone, not being harassed by people.

364
00:36:59,099 --> 00:37:02,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the Buddha would just tell monks just go along with those things, so they will leave you alone.

365
00:37:03,805 --> 00:37:09,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Because otherwise, they're just gonna hassle you and make your life more difficult, which might make your practice more difficult in return.

366
00:37:10,372 --> 00:37:23,566
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, on the other hand, you know, there was an instance when a man came to Buddha and said to accept his daughter as a gift, as an offering to him, because, you know, she was so beautiful and many other teachers would have accepted her, or kings and so on.

367
00:37:23,746 --> 00:37:26,068
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but he wants to give it to Buddha and the Buddha just mocked him.

368
00:37:27,034 --> 00:37:33,937
[SPEAKER_00]: And the whole experience of offering your daughter... and he didn't just... and, you know, and I'm sure majority of people would have agreed with that man.

369
00:37:34,457 --> 00:37:40,419
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, going along with that cannot possibly result in non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

370
00:37:40,519 --> 00:37:45,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot set the example for others to practice toward non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

371
00:37:45,081 --> 00:37:49,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it would have been inappropriate to do so, even if it would have had no desire to that woman.

372
00:37:49,463 --> 00:37:51,584
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... you don't always go by majority.

373
00:37:52,164 --> 00:37:53,705
[SPEAKER_00]: The criteria remains unchanged.

374
00:37:54,125 --> 00:38:08,210
[SPEAKER_00]: What is beneficial is what is rooted in non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, what is rooted in freedom from pressure of desires, what is rooted in dispassion, disenchantment, giving up of the acquisition, simplicity and solitude.

375
00:38:08,810 --> 00:38:10,991
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the criteria that must not be abandoned.

376
00:38:11,611 --> 00:38:14,172
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if sometimes you encounter things in day-to-day life,

377
00:38:15,102 --> 00:38:16,403
[SPEAKER_00]: that you are unsure about.

378
00:38:16,583 --> 00:38:19,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, just just tag them against this this criteria and see.

379
00:38:20,245 --> 00:38:22,127
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you will also see if they're relevant or not.

380
00:38:23,187 --> 00:38:29,431
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like for somebody, like say, a lay practitioner, somebody who is still in the lay setting, but tries to practice the dhamma.

381
00:38:30,632 --> 00:38:35,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, putting up with other people will be will be inevitable.

382
00:38:35,415 --> 00:38:42,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Interactions with them, they demand expectations and then you you might sort of be doubtful as to, you know, how far should I go, what should I do.

383
00:38:42,935 --> 00:38:43,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

384
00:38:43,335 --> 00:38:50,238
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize how relevant things that are asked from you are.

385
00:38:51,078 --> 00:38:54,719
[SPEAKER_00]: How much would they affect your practice, your solitude, your precepts.

386
00:38:55,260 --> 00:38:58,901
[SPEAKER_00]: If they wouldn't really make much difference, you go along with it, so they leave you alone.

387
00:38:59,341 --> 00:39:05,283
[SPEAKER_00]: If they would make a significant difference, like, you know, I don't know, your parents are pressuring you to get married, then you just have to draw a line.

388
00:39:05,403 --> 00:39:09,025
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, even if your whole country and all of your relatives and everybody disagrees with that,

389
00:39:09,705 --> 00:39:16,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the right thing for a man is to marry and start a family, you say no, because the right thing for a man is to not give in to greed, aversion, delusion.

390
00:39:17,750 --> 00:39:20,272
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... and then you take responsibility for it.

391
00:39:20,452 --> 00:39:25,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, if for the rest of your life they don't want to talk to you and dismiss you, it doesn't matter.

392
00:39:25,455 --> 00:39:26,495
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what the truth is.

393
00:39:26,936 --> 00:39:31,979
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense, you're closer to the right criteria of truth, which is non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

394
00:39:31,999 --> 00:39:35,201
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean it will feel like... light, right,

395
00:39:36,181 --> 00:39:43,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Or always justified, you know, undeveloped mind, not free from doubt, will be pressured, will be sort of doubtful, will be trying to kind of sway you.

396
00:39:44,143 --> 00:39:47,984
[SPEAKER_00]: But you stick with the criterias that you now know for yourself are beneficial.

397
00:39:48,644 --> 00:39:50,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it doesn't always feel like that.

398
00:39:50,485 --> 00:39:52,785
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the true practice of going against the grain.

399
00:39:54,265 --> 00:39:59,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so yeah, Kalama Sutta is very actually useful and it can certainly result in in the right view.

400
00:40:00,747 --> 00:40:19,313
[SPEAKER_00]: If applied rightly, if the responsibility is taken up correctly, on the right level, you will not fight the world, but you will not be part of it either, by sort of depending on that kind of existential acceptance and avoidance of responsibility through various sort of externally assumed standpoints.

401
00:40:20,714 --> 00:40:27,736
[SPEAKER_00]: And that in itself is that would constitute what the Buddha was calling the mundane right view, which then, if you cultivate, you will get to see the root of suffering.

402
00:40:28,461 --> 00:40:30,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And the way out, which is pretty much the whole point.

